MARS-OPS Colony Simulator - Person 1 (Core, Simulation, Environment, Main)

This README documents Person 1's part of the MARS-OPS project (Programming II, Lab 5).
Sections that belong to other team members are marked TODO (Person X) so the final README
can be completed by merging everyone's parts.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. System description and main components

MARS-OPS simulates a small colony on Mars. A colony has crew members, robots and
facilities. Time moves forward in steps (called ticks), and in every step the objects in
the colony update themselves. Missions can be submitted, emergencies can be triggered, and
a text report can be printed at any moment.

Person 1 components

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Class                                        Role
  -------------------------------------------- --------------------------------------------
  MarsColony                                   The core class. Holds the crew, robots,
                                               facilities, environment, resource pool,
                                               mission manager and emergency log. Validates
                                               input and delegates work to the other
                                               classes.

  Simulation                                   The clock. Advances time one unit at a time
                                               and sends a tick to the mission manager, the
                                               crew and the robots.

  ColonyReport                                 Builds the text report of the whole colony.
                                               It only reads the colony.

  OperationResult                              The answer to an operation that can fail:
                                               success, or failure with a reason.

  Tickable                                     Interface with onTick(MarsColony).
                                               Implemented by CrewMember, Robot and
                                               MissionManager.

  Environment                                  Stores the current environmental condition
                                               and answers yes/no questions about it.

  EnvironmentCondition                         Enum: NORMAL, DUST_STORM, COMM_DISRUPT.

  Main                                         Builds the colony and runs the demonstration
                                               scenarios (see section 9).
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Components written by other team members (used, not written, by Person 1)

Agent, CrewMember, Robot, Facility, ResourcePool, ResourceType, MissionManager, Mission,
Emergency and its subclasses.

UML class diagram (Person 1 part)

(See the file UML_Person1.png - the UML class diagram.)

Blue boxes are Person 1 classes. Grey boxes are teammates' classes, shown only by name.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Design decisions and assumptions (Person 1)

Aggregation, not composition, for the colony's parts. MarsColony receives Environment,
ResourcePool and MissionManager through its constructor. It does not create them, and they
could exist without the colony, so the relationship is aggregation (hollow diamond). Crew,
robots and facilities are also aggregation: the lists and the objects inside them are
created outside and passed in. The same applies to the Emergency objects kept in the log:
the colony creates the empty log list, but each emergency is created outside and handed to
triggerEmergency.

The lists are shared, not copied. The constructor stores the same list objects it receives
(this.crew = crew). This is needed so that ResourcePool and MarsColony see the same
facilities list: a facility added later through addfacility must also be visible to the
resource pool. Because of this, the lists passed in must be mutable (new ArrayList<>(),
not List.of(...) or Arrays.asList(...)).

Read-only getters, controlled changes. getCrew(), getRobots(), getFacilities() and
getEmergencyLog() return unmodifiable views. Outside code can read and loop over them but
cannot add or remove directly. All additions go through addcrew, addrobot and addfacility,
which reject null.

Two ways of reporting problems. - A programmer mistake (for example passing null where an
object is required) throws IllegalArgumentException. - An expected failure (for example a
mission that is rejected) returns an OperationResult with isSuccess() == false and a
readable reason.

Delegation. MarsColony does not contain mission, resource or emergency rules. getTotal
asks ResourcePool, submitMission asks MissionManager, triggerEmergency asks the Emergency
to handle itself, and generateReport asks ColonyReport.

OperationResult has a private constructor. Results can only be created with
OperationResult.ok() or OperationResult.fail(reason). A failure with a missing or blank
reason gets a default message, so a failure always has a reason.

Environment is not Tickable. The environment does not change by itself over time. It
changes only when Main calls setCondition(...).

Assumptions

-   Teammates' classes follow the shared contracts: Agent has getName(), isAvailable(),
    isOperational(), getLocation(); CrewMember and Robot implement Agent and Tickable;
    MissionManager implements Tickable and has submit, getActiveMission, getHistory; etc.
-   All classes are in the same package (person1) or are imported correctly (see section
    8).
-   ResourcePool keeps the same facilities list object it receives.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Resource allocation rules

Person 1's part: MarsColony.getTotal(ResourceType) forwards the question to ResourcePool,
and ColonyReport prints the total of every ResourceType in the "RESOURCE TOTALS" section.
MarsColony itself does not allocate, reserve or consume resources.

TODO (Person 3): document the allocation rules here (how resources are reserved, consumed
and released; what happens when there is not enough; how facilities affect totals).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Emergency rules

Person 1's part: MarsColony.triggerEmergency(Emergency): 1. rejects null with an
IllegalArgumentException; 2. adds the emergency to emergencyLog first, so it stays on
record; 3. calls emergency.handle(this), passing the colony so the emergency can act on
it.

ColonyReport prints every emergency in the log under "EMERGENCY OUTCOMES": its type, its
state, and its reason when one exists.

TODO (Person who wrote Emergency and its subclasses): document each emergency type, what
triggers it, what it changes in the colony, and its possible states and outcomes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Environmental effects

Person 1 defines the environment (class Environment, enum EnvironmentCondition).

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Condition         isDustStorm()     isCommsDisrupted()   allowsSurfaceOperations()   allowsRemoteControl()
  ----------------- ----------------- -------------------- --------------------------- -----------------------
  NORMAL            false             false                true                        true

  DUST_STORM        true              false                false                       true

  COMM_DISRUPT      false             true                 true                        false
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-   The condition starts as NORMAL.
-   setCondition(null) is ignored and the old condition stays.
-   Only one condition is active at a time.
-   The condition changes only when Main calls setCondition(...). It does not change over
    time.
-   Other classes must ask allowsSurfaceOperations() and allowsRemoteControl() instead of
    comparing enum values themselves.

TODO (Persons 2 and 4): document exactly what each effect does to robots, crew and
missions (for example which missions are blocked or fail during a dust storm).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Mission states

Person 1's part: MarsColony.submitMission(mission, agents) rejects a null mission or a
null agent list with a failed OperationResult. Otherwise it passes the request to
MissionManager.submit(...) and returns its answer unchanged.

ColonyReport shows the active mission (name, state, progress as elapsed/duration, and the
failure reason if there is one) and the mission history (name, state, and the reason if
any).

TODO (Person 2 / Person 4): list every mission state, what moves a mission from one state
to the next, and when a mission fails.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. Other implementation decisions left open by the specification

-   Tick order. In each time unit Simulation does: (1) time + 1, (2) update the colony's
    time, (3) tick MissionManager, (4) tick every crew member, (5) tick every robot. The
    mission manager goes first so it sees the agents as they were before they update in
    the same unit. Facilities and the environment are not ticked.
-   Who owns the time. The colony stores the simulation time. Simulation reads it from the
    colony, adds 1 and writes it back, so the report always shows the current time.
-   Negative values. advanceTime(units) with a negative number and setSimulationTime(t)
    with a negative number throw IllegalArgumentException. advanceTime(0) does nothing.
-   Report format. Sections in this order: time, environment, resource totals, crew,
    robots, facilities, active mission, mission history, emergency outcomes. An unknown
    location is printed as Unknown. An empty section prints "No active mission.", "No
    missions recorded." or "No emergencies recorded." A failure reason is printed only
    when it exists and is not empty.
-   getAvailableAgents(). An agent is available only if it is both isAvailable() and
    isOperational().
-   Method names. The add methods are addcrew, addrobot, addfacility (lowercase). Make
    sure every class that calls them uses the same spelling.
-   Known limitations. The add methods do not reject duplicates (the same object could be
    added twice and then ticked twice). ColonyReport builds text with String +=, which is
    simple but less efficient than StringBuilder.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8. Compiling and running

Requirements: JDK 17 or newer.

From the root of the repository, compile every source file together into an out folder:

    javac -d out person1/*.java person2/*.java person3/*.java person4/*.java

Run the program:

    java -cp out person1.Main

Notes: - This works when all classes declare the same package (package person1;) or import
each other correctly. If the team uses different packages, MarsColony needs import lines
for the teammates' classes. - Main.java is in the person1 folder. If it is in a different
folder or package, change the run command to match (for example java -cp out Main).

TODO (team): confirm the final folder and package layout before submission and update
these commands if needed.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

9. Running the demonstration scenarios

Main builds the colony and runs the scenarios in order, printing the report before and
after the important steps. The general flow of every scenario is:

1.  Create mutable lists: new ArrayList<>() for crew, robots and facilities.
2.  Create Environment, ResourcePool (passing the same facilities list) and MissionManager
    using the constructors agreed by the team.
3.  Create the MarsColony and add crew, robots and facilities with addcrew, addrobot,
    addfacility.
4.  Print the starting state: System.out.println(colony.generateReport());
5.  Create Simulation sim = new Simulation(colony);
6.  Apply the scenario: change the environment with
    colony.getEnvironment().setCondition(...), submit missions with
    colony.submitMission(...), trigger emergencies with colony.triggerEmergency(...).
7.  Advance time with sim.advanceTime(n).
8.  Print the report again to show the result.

